#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425882} :activity/id #uuid "b3d34df6-7ccc-52f2-8398-0ea592619405" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed9d04a-f96a-5183-9fc4-8dcfd77061e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425858} {:answer/id #uuid "56a2e6b5-5884-5570-aa94-176a32aa11de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425860} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1ffe30-2504-50cd-8a45-20ca6353d983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425863} {:answer/id #uuid "9778e0bb-2590-5c24-908d-c9bf4daf3a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425864} {:answer/id #uuid "da2916dc-97c4-51c9-852e-307c5a102816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425868} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c3bd452-520c-5cac-8263-6875362cc4e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ea583ed-35c3-538b-b597-43518611b1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-77072" :question/problem "![](images/01/014c2310bcac5b6e9c2be2107bf8a5613b689ff29a8018d2be74fc607dd996db.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨7-2{t}^{3},3{t}^{2}+10⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7-2{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+10)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6{t}^{2},\\,6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-6{t}^{2})^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}6t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+1$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}3\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2t\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2(8-1) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1eeb87c5-827d-5f16-8c07-70b850a6644d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394cf326-2a8e-53d3-b6bb-9d00bbd00896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425857} {:answer/id #uuid "cdaba3a2-4f44-5d67-97c9-41759abf06af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425866} {:answer/id #uuid "d00a3088-dd85-511b-a78d-c803fdd41730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425867} {:answer/id #uuid "e302e086-65fb-5d1c-95c1-38d5bea87093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425869} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0c8f0c-3314-5bf3-83d0-87976ede682e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425867} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed337fba-8f9c-5284-b664-f31a93279b80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eec69fbf-a0cd-50e6-9b17-0f0c96351008" :question/math-academy-id "q-69953" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "The $1$st row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+1\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "40454b93-13bb-56b8-b1f8-96f146abc4d9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd9cf94f-6d2c-5279-b476-55c7434eefef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303725} {:answer/id #uuid "06570ee4-ef32-50c5-80dd-d62ee7471554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8xe^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425856} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37a43fd-f6c9-58a0-8e79-34180df6e0a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f0a9b7d-a8ca-55a3-9a38-83e9ea72a8f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-332730" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "518701d8-bb48-5e81-82cd-b420231888d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eedb5eb-4a53-52bf-bb91-736a768c01a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425859} {:answer/id #uuid "5796bd74-a234-55f5-a5f9-bee4c20f2c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425861} {:answer/id #uuid "6e08cddb-71c7-54e2-99d5-5e60caa26d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425862} {:answer/id #uuid "c53010b8-a034-5aca-8828-82b8098d48fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425865} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8ef8d9-7011-5f21-8ec2-2534d66acfdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425861} :answer-field/id #uuid "375ee444-4376-5150-bb9c-79b09c44be67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2365a6d7-b820-5041-9972-25df8cfa8866" :question/math-academy-id "q-86922" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\nI. The outcome of rolling a $10$-sided die\n\nII. The number of aces in a standard deck of playing cards\n\nIII. The weight of a randomly selected person, measured in kilograms" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,10,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The number of aces in a standard deck of playing cards is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $4$ aces in a standard deck of playing cards.)\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b8cfa482-64ec-5b16-91c4-308e96891613" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61c1ce28-8bfe-5b09-a15d-7c4b76ecfa92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309490} {:answer/id #uuid "92b108b0-3b47-556c-89ac-daa66d2c979e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} {:answer/id #uuid "a553f444-e68c-53b4-8d93-575a4cc7cf15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310080} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fbe008-7f1a-5a31-9cb7-f9dd2c67fa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310318} {:answer/id #uuid "ec46c1a1-e0b3-5094-a3be-1a97aa9a9b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309920} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e1bdc7f-51da-5393-bc6a-4b97c1c1ced8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a1235f2-6c7a-5f14-b04f-a38d354a3d47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337837" :question/problem "Given that $D=2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=2x,{f}_{1}=3,$ and ${f}_{0}=-2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =(2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{3}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bb759bf3-e1d6-55c4-ad0e-7fff246f2577" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425881}}] :activity/title "Quiz 4" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425855}]}
